# Role
You are an expert in vehicle brand and model naming conventions across manufacturers and markets.

# Task
Two vehicle descriptions `x` and `y` are given below, serialized as `col1: val1, col2: val2, …`. They are constructed so that they differ only in the value of the `{{attribute}}` field.

Determine whether the two `{{attribute}}` values refer to the **same real-world {{attribute}} entity** — that is, whether they are two surface forms of a single entity.

# Criterion
Return `is_match: true` ONLY when the two `{{attribute}}` values are **lexical variants of the same real-world entity**:
- Abbreviations:          "vw" ↔ "volkswagen", "chevy" ↔ "chevrolet", "mb" ↔ "mercedes-benz".
- Hyphenation / spacing:  "alfa romeo" ↔ "alfa-romeo", "c 350" ↔ "c350", "t-roc" ↔ "t roc", "f-150" ↔ "f150".
- Localized translations: "serie 3" ↔ "3 series", "classe e" ↔ "e class".
- Catalog-canonical vs listing-written form of the same entity (e.g. "corsa" ↔ "opel corsa" when the brand prefix is stripped).

Return `is_match: false` in **every other case**, including:
- Different brands — even if under the same parent group, on the same platform, or the same group's discontinued/successor pairs:
  - GMC ≠ Chevrolet (both GM), Saturn ≠ Buick (both GM), Mercury ≠ Ford (Mercury was Ford's defunct marque but a distinct brand), Dodge ≠ Ram (split in 2010), Skoda ≠ Volkswagen (VW Group), Fiat ≠ Alfa Romeo (Stellantis), Dacia ≠ Renault.
- Different models with no shared name root (Sierra ≠ Silverado; Dokker ≠ Express; Logan ≠ Siena).
- Models that share a name root but denote distinct vehicles:
  - Body/style variants:  Golf GTI ≠ Golf; 308 SW ≠ 308; Corolla Cross ≠ Corolla; Xsara Picasso ≠ Xsara; Transit Connect ≠ Transit Custom.
  - Size/wheelbase variants:  Grand Cherokee L ≠ Grand Cherokee; XC40 ≠ XC60; CLA ≠ GLA.
  - Performance variants: TTS ≠ TT; Golf R ≠ Golf; M3 ≠ 3 Series.
  - Same name root, different platforms: Xceed ≠ Ceed; 500X ≠ 500; Corolla ≠ Corolla Verso.
- Alphanumeric designations that denote different engines/trims of otherwise-same body but are listed as distinct models (e.g. "e 320" ≠ "320" because they are from different brands: Mercedes E-Class vs BMW 3 Series).

# Important rule
The other attributes (year, mileage, color, body_type, engine, transmission, …) in `x` and `y` may match or differ. **They are irrelevant to this decision.** Two listings with identical year, mileage, engine, and color may still describe vehicles of different brands or different models. Base your answer ONLY on whether the two `{{attribute}}` values are surface forms of the same entity.

# Inputs
x: "{{x}}"
y: "{{y}}"

